  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research study · draft · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      RCE Compile: A Law of Compilation, Tested as a Normal Form Against Real Compilers
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      RCE Compile is a research project in the repository of the Reality Code Engine (RCE). It asks whether one statement of what compilation is, a Law of Compilation, holds for any compiler, independent of RCE. Checked against twelve families of production compilers, the candidate fails as a description: no compiler hashes a name-free representation, lowering in shipping compilers is neither total nor proved to preserve meaning, and no compiler accounts for energy. It holds as a normative framework: a normal form with two bounds and a measured distance from them, against which every compiler can be placed.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">What the study claims and what it does not</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>It claims that the candidate, as first stated, is falsified as a description of real compilers, and gives the evidence part by part.</li>
        <li>It states a refined standard with seven parts, four equivalence levels and a gate that names what it preserves and the evidence it accepts on, and seven hypotheses with falsifiers.</li>
        <li>It reports the three empirical tracks run so far. Byte comparisons of compiler outputs on three machines leave the hypothesis that lowering is deterministic neither supported nor falsified. Alongside the original 96-row WebAssembly erasure counts, pre-registered Linux RAPL on-chip <code>reported_j</code> measurements across 20 deterministic kernels and 15 compiler tiers (300 of 300 checksum-verified) show that lowering from Python to <code>clang -O3</code> pays back after a median of 2,490 runs, <code>-O0</code> to <code>-O3</code> needs 53,464 to 102,720 runs, <code>-O2</code> to <code>-O3</code> never recovers its extra compile joules on 4 to 6 of the 20 kernels, pre-compiling RCE's standalone Rust runtime prelude once into a shared <code>librce_rt.rlib</code> crate cuts per-kernel emitted Rust <code>-O3</code> compile <code>reported_j</code> by 20.07× (56.29 J to 2.81 J core, cross-checked at 15.44× on an idle dedicated Core i9-13900H host) and lowers its payback against Node JS from 31,080 to 1,079 runs, and Regime-B time multiplied by power explains far more variance than static IR size (0.9999 against 0.1154). De Bruijn name-free hashing scaled across 185 RCE Forms, 60 C/Rust/Python kernels, and 500 verified semantic mutants yields 0 of 500 (0.00%) false hits and 167.8× to 448.5× warm rebuild savings, whereas naive identifier-stripping collides on 150 of 150 variable-binding swaps.</li>
        <li>It does not claim board-level supply wattmeter readings (<code>board_metered = false</code>, <code>measured_j = None</code>), Regime-A or Regime-C hardware measurements, a measured 98/2 split, or priority over earlier work: two primary sources (Hoare, He and Sampaio, 1993; Ershov, 1977) have not yet been read in full, and almost every external source was seen only as a search result.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

